    <statement><p>Find the arc length of the graph of the function 
<m>f(x)=5 \sqrt{x^{3}}</m>
from <m>x= 4</m> to <m>x=8</m>.</p>
<p>arc length = <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>73.2474552982187</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>5 x^{3/2}</m> in disguise.  So
the arc length is 
<me>\int_{4}^{8}\,\sqrt{1 + (f'(x))^2}\,dx = 
  \int_{4}^{8}\,\sqrt{1 + ({15\over 2} x^{1/2})^2}\,dx = 
  \int_{4}^{8}\,\sqrt{1 + {225\over 4} x}\,dx.</me>
With <m>w = 1 + {225\over 4} x</m>, this becomes 
<me>\int_{4}^{8}\,\sqrt{1 + {225\over 4} x}\,dx = 
  {{4\over {225\over 4}}} \int_{226}^{451}\,\sqrt{w}\,dw = 
  {{8\over 0}} ( 451^{3/2} - 226^{3/2} ).</me>



</solution>
